{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1774026701,"nonce":"TAYbl0osNPXEZcBb6Uq5W/zcKac=","message":"**Round 1 synthesis — activation + hierarchy alignment**\n\nTwo independent analyses (Opus + Gemini) converged on:\n\n**Activation timing**: Both propose two-phase: early signal at worktree creation (`wt --issue \u003cid\u003e`), definitive activation at first commit (`agent-commit` auto-discovers from `.issue` file). The original reasoning for commit-only activation (\"agent-commit is already enforced\") was sound for a simpler model, but the exploration gap (10-30 min before first commit) matters for visibility and duplicate-dispatch prevention.\n\n**Hierarchy**: Both propose a `work_units` reconciliation table that joins issue tree (git-bug), branch tree (__-naming), and merge tree (git history). `parent_branch` derived from `__` parsing. `kind` (leaf/integration/solo) derived from children. The mapping is structural, not conventional.\n\n**`.issue` file**: Gemini's standout contribution — `wt --issue \u003cid\u003e` writes a `.issue` file in the worktree root, `agent-commit` auto-discovers it. Declare once, use everywhere. Highest-leverage single change.\n\n**Implementation order agreed**: (1) .issue file, (2) work_units table, (3) wt registration, (4) inbox UI, (5) wt-merge status updates, (6) auth request auto-collects child issues.","files":null}]}